{"rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and, in the text field, an original fictional passage of about 250 words that comes immediately AFTER the current text in the same invented story.", "text": "Mara lunged before she could think. She clapped her palm over his mouth, and the note he'd begun buzzed against her skin like a trapped bee. His breath was ice. \"Don't,\" she begged. \"Toby, please, it's not her. It's not Mam.\" His eyes were open but empty, fixed on something past her shoulder. Beneath her hand, his lips kept moving. Wenna crossed the room in three strides, faster than Mara had ever seen her move. She pulled a pouch from her apron and upended it into her palm: salt, grey and coarse, flecked with something dark that smelled of iron. \"Hold him,\" she said. \"Whatever happens, you hold him.\" She pressed the salt against the blackened thread. It hissed. Tobias arched in Mara's arms, and a sound tore out of him that was not his own voice, too deep and too many at once, like a choir heard underwater. The candles guttered sideways, all toward the door. Outside, something laughed. \"Salt and old words,\" the voice said. \"You kept me out once, Wenna Hale. You were younger then. You had more to give.\" Wenna's jaw tightened. \"I have enough.\" The thread snapped. Tobias went limp, gasping, and the green light beneath his skin guttered out. Mara clutched him to her chest, sobbing into his hair. But when she looked up, Wenna was staring at her own wrist. There, pale and thin as a spider's line, a new red thread had begun to wind itself around her bones."}
